WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:06.240
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:14.030
Hello, I'm Randy Haberkamp.

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:16.490
I'm the Senior Vice
President of Preservation

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:19.340
and Foundation Programs for the
Academy of Motion Picture Arts

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:23.150
and Sciences, and this
is Hollywood Home Movies:

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:25.160
The Virtual Edition!

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:29.030
Some of you may have been to
our Hollywood Home Movies events

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:30.920
at the Linwood Dunn
Theater, or seen us

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:34.190
at various film festivals
or other presentations.

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:34.890
That's great.

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:38.090
Others of you are going, this
is the Academy - what are they

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:39.920
doing with these home movies?

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:41.270
Uncle Joe used to show those.

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:42.750
I don't want to
see those anymore.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:43.670
They're boring.

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:44.540
Trust me.

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:46.570
This is going to be
a really fun show,

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:50.960
and not only will you see some
home movies of Hollywood stars

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.440
and Hollywood behind the
scenes, directors and producers,

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:57.590
but you'll also, I think,
get a different appreciation

00:00:57.590 --> 00:00:59.570
for home movies,
because there are

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:03.230
some really wonderful things
that happen in home movies that

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:05.150
aren't around anywhere else.

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:06.830
And I'd like to
introduce you now

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:09.230
to the person who's
curated this show, as well

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:11.750
as all of the home movie
programs that we've done.

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:13.940
Please welcome Lynne Kirste.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:15.150
Lynne, good to see you.

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:17.150
Thanks for putting
this together for us.

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:17.840
Hey, Randy.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:20.460
Great to see you,
and hi, everybody.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.150
I love curating the home movie
collection at the Academy,

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:25.730
and I'm thrilled that
we have this opportunity

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:27.980
to share some of these
wonderful films with you.

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:31.550
I think of home movies as being
kind of like a time machine,

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:35.360
because they allow us to
experience the past in a really

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:38.990
personal, intimate way that
no other kind of film does.

00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:40.670
While these are
Hollywood Home Movies,

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:42.650
the Academy has
collected thousands

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:45.590
of reels that were shot
by all sorts of people who

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:49.610
use their amateur cameras to
document their lives, families,

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:51.980
and experiences,
and in home movies

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:55.640
we can often see content
and points of view

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:58.550
that are frequently left
out in mainstream film.

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:01.380
The Academy does take home
movies very seriously.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.035
We think they're
important and we

00:02:03.035 --> 00:02:05.660
think they're an important part
of our motion picture heritage.

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:07.340
This program will
give us a glimpse,

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:09.740
as Randy said, into
some Hollywood folks

00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:14.810
at work and at play from
the 1930s into the 1960s,

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:18.350
and as you may know, Hollywood
was not a very inclusive place

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:19.490
during those years.

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:22.430
But while it's gotten better,
there's still a long way to go.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:24.590
But even back then,
there were some people

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:28.100
who overcame barriers against
women, people of color,

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:31.460
and other groups to work
in a variety of positions

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:33.390
in Hollywood and beyond.

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:37.920
So this program will feature and
celebrate some of those people,

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:41.000
And I think that they
still inspire us today.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:43.520
All the home movies in
the program are silent,

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:46.440
so Randy and I will
provide commentary,

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:48.440
and we have a couple of
special guests coming up

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:50.420
who will join us
a little later on.

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:53.900
And right now we have one
other important collaborator

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:55.700
who Randy will introduce.

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:59.180
So home movies were
traditionally done with just

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:02.300
a projector and a screen, but
we wanted to add a little bit

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:05.720
of a professional flair, so
we're going to do like they did

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:10.070
in the silent days before sound
came in - in the early '20s.

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:12.140
We're going to have live
musical accompaniment,

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:15.830
so please welcome our special
guest, the twinkling ivories

00:03:15.830 --> 00:03:17.900
of Michael Mortilla.

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:22.550
Hey, kids, nice
of you to drop by.

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:24.320
Thanks for joining us, Michael.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:26.720
I think the stage is set.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:28.780
We've got the movies loaded up.

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:30.260
Michael, take it away.

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:36.080
So here we go.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:38.650
We're going to start out with
some footage from the Florenz

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:41.770
Ziegfeld-Billie
Burke collection.

00:03:41.770 --> 00:03:45.820
This is Catalina Island in
1934, and folks from Hollywood

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:49.210
loved to sail over to Catalina
and spend the weekend anchored,

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:50.335
hanging out in their boats.

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:56.060
Here come a couple now.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:59.420
This is lesbian director
Dorothy Arzner on the right

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:03.530
and actress Billie
Burke on the left.

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:06.170
Arzner was the only woman
directing films in Hollywood

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:08.570
at this point in time.

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:11.690
She had directed her
first film in 1927

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:14.720
and was the only woman director
in Hollywood for many years.

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:17.450
She and Burke were
quite good friends,

00:04:17.450 --> 00:04:20.959
and this is Burke's
daughter, Patricia Ziegfeld,

00:04:20.959 --> 00:04:23.870
in the beret, and
her friend Blackie.

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:25.910
Sadly, her father,
Florenz Ziegfeld

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:27.260
had died a few years earlier.

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:34.820
Here's Arzner with her
Kodak Jiffy still camera.

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:38.000
She started working in
the film industry in 1919

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.110
as an editor and a writer, and
then later became a director,

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:44.750
and was the first woman to be a
member of the Directors Guild.

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:49.890
Here's Blackie again, probably
filmed by Patricia or Billie

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:50.390
Burke.

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:51.765
The two of them
were the ones who

00:04:51.765 --> 00:04:53.990
shot this home movie and
many other home movies that

00:04:53.990 --> 00:04:55.384
are in their collection.

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:02.970
And that's Blackie's
husband with one

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:05.160
of the great white
steamers behind him.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:08.340
These were the ferry boats that
took people from the mainland

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:09.990
over to Catalina
Island and back.

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:18.380
So Hollywood folks loved to
come over and sail around.

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:20.648
Sometimes they'd paddle
over to visit people

00:05:20.648 --> 00:05:22.190
on each other's
boats, like these two

00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:26.240
women seem to have just done, or
they'd go fishing or swimming.

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:28.890
Here goes Patricia diving in.

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:32.410
We'll see her mother in
the water a little later.

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:33.490
I love that nose guard.

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:39.290
Billie Burke is probably
best known today

00:05:39.290 --> 00:05:42.590
for playing Glinda the Good
Witch in The Wizard of Oz,

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:44.810
and she made dozens and
dozens of other movies.

00:05:48.325 --> 00:05:49.700
Now this is another
day and we're

00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:51.290
heading out of
Los Angeles harbor

00:05:51.290 --> 00:05:54.002
to visit the Hussar, which
is this enormous yacht.

00:05:54.002 --> 00:05:56.210
It was the largest privately
owned yacht in the world

00:05:56.210 --> 00:05:59.868
at this time, four
masts, and coming up I

00:05:59.868 --> 00:06:01.910
want you to look really
quickly at the right hand

00:06:01.910 --> 00:06:04.503
side of your screen for
costume director Adrian.

00:06:04.503 --> 00:06:06.170
He's going to show
up for just a second.

00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:10.707
There he is, and here
again Arzner and Burke.

00:06:10.707 --> 00:06:13.040
And that was Sam Goldwyn with
them, and the four of them

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:16.490
are all going over
to visit the Hussar.

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:19.123
Adrian had designed costumes
for two of Arzner's films,

00:06:19.123 --> 00:06:20.540
and he worked on
a number of films

00:06:20.540 --> 00:06:23.002
that Burke appeared in,
including Dinner at Eight

00:06:23.002 --> 00:06:24.335
and The Wizard of Oz, of course.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:29.390
So you may be
thinking whoever owns

00:06:29.390 --> 00:06:30.830
this yacht must
be pretty wealthy,

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:32.270
and you would be right.

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:37.010
It's E.F. Hutton and
Marjorie Merriweather Post.

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:41.860
Post actually designed the
interiors of the yacht herself.

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:42.860
And it just keeps going.

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:53.040
And now we're back over at
Catalina in Avalon Harbor.

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:58.180
This is Patricia's
boyfriend, and there's Burke

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:00.650
in the water taking a dip.

00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:03.190
Billie Burke actually
started off as a stage actor,

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:06.710
and she was very celebrated
in the early 1900s,

00:07:06.710 --> 00:07:09.800
and then she had a whole second
career as a popular character

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:12.290
actress in Hollywood.

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:16.010
Dorothy Arzner directed
films into the early '40s

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:19.070
and then during World War II
she directed training films

00:07:19.070 --> 00:07:21.140
for the Women's Army Corps.

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:24.200
And later on she joined
the faculty at UCLA

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:27.500
and taught filmmaking there.

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:30.650
We are indebted to our friends
at the UCLA Film and Television

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:33.515
Archive for restoring many
of Arzner's feature films.

00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:41.941
And that's our little look
at Billie Burke and Dorothy

00:07:41.941 --> 00:07:42.441
Arzner.

00:07:46.050 --> 00:07:47.950
It's really great
footage to see, Lynne.

00:07:47.950 --> 00:07:51.080
I love seeing Catalina, but
I think, if I'm not mistaken,

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:53.310
we've got some color
footage coming up, as well,

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:55.280
so that's even more exciting.

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:56.240
Yes, we do.

00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:59.420
The next home movie
is Catalina in 1935,

00:07:59.420 --> 00:08:03.470
shot in early Kodachrome
by actor Gilbert Roland,

00:08:03.470 --> 00:08:06.710
and this film has some
footage of William Haines

00:08:06.710 --> 00:08:11.210
who was a very popular actor in
the late '20s and early '30s,

00:08:11.210 --> 00:08:14.240
but who had just recently been
blackballed by the Hollywood

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:15.380
studios.

00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:17.970
So 1935 was a turning
point in his career.

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:21.740
This is when he launched his
second career as a designer

00:08:21.740 --> 00:08:23.930
and decorator to the stars.

00:08:23.930 --> 00:08:25.610
And what happened
with William Haines

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:30.020
is that he was openly gay and
was in a longtime relationship,

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:34.010
and MGM in 1933
decided that he had

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:37.130
to enter into a phony
heterosexual marriage for PR

00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:38.059
purposes.

00:08:38.059 --> 00:08:41.690
Well, Haines refused to
do it, and that was it.

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:43.580
He was blackballed
and he was never

00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:45.950
able to get another
acting job, so he

00:08:45.950 --> 00:08:47.570
turned to designing
and decorating

00:08:47.570 --> 00:08:50.013
and had a very successful
second career at it.

00:08:50.013 --> 00:08:51.680
So let's have a look
at this home movie.

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:54.890
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:08:54.890 --> 00:08:57.130
So here we go with
actor Gilbert Roland.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:06.390
That's George Cukor in the
white shirt, David O. Selznick,

00:09:06.390 --> 00:09:08.670
and Billy Haines is standing
up in the striped shirt.

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:16.740
George Cukor was a good
friend of Haines' and he

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:18.630
was known for throwing
a lot of really

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:22.380
big, gay parties in
Hollywood, and he actually

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:24.690
had Haines to
redecorate his house.

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:26.730
That was one of Haines's
first commissions,

00:09:26.730 --> 00:09:30.150
and he turned it into a real
showplace for Cukor's parties.

00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:32.420
Selznick loved also
to throw parties

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:34.170
and he's our host for
this yachting party.

00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:41.780
So William Haines was in
this longtime relationship

00:09:41.780 --> 00:09:44.060
and it turned out to be
a lifetime partnership

00:09:44.060 --> 00:09:48.650
with his partner, Jimmy Shields,
and they worked together

00:09:48.650 --> 00:09:50.960
over the years when they
started the design business,

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:52.495
and became very successful.

00:09:52.495 --> 00:09:54.620
In fact, he's much better
known today as a designer

00:09:54.620 --> 00:09:57.250
than he was as an actor.

00:09:57.250 --> 00:09:58.815
Here's Gilbert Roland.

00:09:58.815 --> 00:10:01.190
He's handed the camera to
someone else for just a moment,

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:02.470
so he can get in the picture.

00:10:02.470 --> 00:10:05.380
He's usually behind the
camera in his home movies.

00:10:05.380 --> 00:10:06.025
He's so shy.

00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:12.990
This is Irene Mayer Selznick
who is married to David

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:18.420
and later became a noted
theatrical producer,

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:20.970
and here we have a couple
of Academy Award winners.

00:10:20.970 --> 00:10:23.880
That's screenwriter Herman
Mankiewicz on the left,

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:27.630
and director Lewis
Milestone on the right.

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:30.495
Mankiewicz co-wrote Citizen
Kane and many other classics.

00:10:40.690 --> 00:10:42.930
There he goes, Tarzan
diving into the water.

00:10:42.930 --> 00:10:45.408
That's Johnny Weissmuller.

00:10:45.408 --> 00:10:46.950
As we saw in the
previous home movie,

00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:48.950
people would go visit
each other on their yachts

00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:51.240
and their boats, and they
usually would actually

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:53.790
come over in a boat,
but Weissmuller just

00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:55.624
paddles over on his longboard.

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:09.520
And here's Constance Bennett
on the left waving to us.

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:11.590
She was very popular
in the early '30s

00:11:11.590 --> 00:11:14.830
and was constantly on the
cover of movie magazines.

00:11:14.830 --> 00:11:17.140
She and Gilbert Roland were
married about a few years

00:11:17.140 --> 00:11:18.430
after this film was shot.

00:11:18.430 --> 00:11:21.300
They were already a romantic
couple at this time,

00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:24.710
and they had two
daughters together.

00:11:24.710 --> 00:11:27.490
Gilbert Roland
was born in Mexico

00:11:27.490 --> 00:11:29.570
and he grew up in
El Paso, Texas.

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:36.660
Actually, his given name
was Luis Antonio de Alonso,

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:38.910
but he changed it to his
screen name, Gilbert Roland,

00:11:38.910 --> 00:11:42.420
based on two of his favorite
actors, John Gilbert and Ruth

00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:43.800
Roland.

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:46.140
And he came to
Hollywood as a teenager

00:11:46.140 --> 00:11:48.750
and started acting
in silent films,

00:11:48.750 --> 00:11:51.540
and worked all the
way up till 1982.

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:53.790
There go Cukor and Haines again.

00:11:53.790 --> 00:11:56.550
Haines also decorated
Constance Bennett's house

00:11:56.550 --> 00:11:58.980
and also redid her sister
Joan Bennett's house,

00:11:58.980 --> 00:12:01.230
as well as many other
Hollywood folks.

00:12:03.790 --> 00:12:05.385
Bennett, as well as
being an actress,

00:12:05.385 --> 00:12:06.885
had her own line
of beauty products.

00:12:10.297 --> 00:12:11.880
Now Gilbert Roland
originally intended

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:14.430
to be a matador like his
father and grandfather,

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:16.800
but then he fell in love with
movies, came to Hollywood

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:20.010
and first worked as an extra,
and then became a fine actor

00:12:20.010 --> 00:12:21.810
and also shot some
great home movies.

00:12:30.390 --> 00:12:32.970
So our next home movie is
also from Gilbert Roland's

00:12:32.970 --> 00:12:35.730
collection, and this one,
he's behind the camera.

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:38.760
That's Dolores Del Rio on the
right talking to Irene Mayer

00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:40.140
Selznick.

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:44.040
Del Rio had come to Hollywood
from her native Mexico in 1925

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:47.200
and quickly became
very successful.

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:50.530
King Vidor with
the tennis racket,

00:12:50.530 --> 00:12:54.953
perhaps best known for directing
The Big Parade or Hallelujah.

00:12:59.590 --> 00:13:01.300
And this is Elizabeth Allen.

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:04.930
She had recently acted in
films like David Copperfield

00:13:04.930 --> 00:13:06.242
and A Tale of Two Cities.

00:13:06.242 --> 00:13:08.200
There are so many great
clothes in home movies.

00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:10.540
That's another thing about
them that's really wonderful.

00:13:10.540 --> 00:13:14.720
Now here's a great picture
of John Gilbert, who was also

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:18.440
a great actor and, of
course, one of the people

00:13:18.440 --> 00:13:20.715
that Gilbert Roland
took his name from.

00:13:20.715 --> 00:13:22.340
And this footage is
kind of bittersweet

00:13:22.340 --> 00:13:25.190
because John Gilbert passed
away about six months

00:13:25.190 --> 00:13:26.780
after this home movie was shot.

00:13:26.780 --> 00:13:30.750
This may be the last moving
image footage of him.

00:13:30.750 --> 00:13:32.630
There's David O.
Selznick with King Vidor

00:13:32.630 --> 00:13:35.030
and our hostess, Del
Rio, in the background.

00:13:35.030 --> 00:13:37.670
The three of them had all worked
on the film Bird of Paradise

00:13:37.670 --> 00:13:38.510
a few years earlier.

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:44.535
John Gilbert again and
now dressed in white.

00:13:44.535 --> 00:13:46.910
Del Rio had introduced Gilbert
to her good friend Marlene

00:13:46.910 --> 00:13:49.580
Dietrich, and Gilbert and
Dietrich were now a couple.

00:13:58.620 --> 00:14:02.130
So Del Rio was obviously one
of the most beautiful actresses

00:14:02.130 --> 00:14:05.850
in Hollywood, and she was
a huge star at this time,

00:14:05.850 --> 00:14:09.390
but she eventually got tired of
the kind of glamor girl parts

00:14:09.390 --> 00:14:11.280
that she was offered
in Hollywood.

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:13.800
And she really wanted to
stretch herself as an actress,

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:17.365
so she went back to Mexico
where she had a range of roles

00:14:17.365 --> 00:14:19.740
and became a really important
figure in the establishment

00:14:19.740 --> 00:14:20.948
of the Mexican film industry.

00:14:31.820 --> 00:14:34.340
Here's Gary Cooper
in his mohair suit.

00:14:39.312 --> 00:14:41.770
He and Dietrich will soon start
working on the film Desire.

00:14:52.430 --> 00:14:56.730
And here's our host, Cedric
Gibbons, MGM art director.

00:14:56.730 --> 00:14:58.860
He had a really amazing career.

00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:04.200
He was nominated for 39
Oscars and he won 11 times.

00:15:04.200 --> 00:15:05.800
And in addition
to winning Oscars,

00:15:05.800 --> 00:15:08.389
he is the person who
designed the Oscar statuette.

00:15:14.380 --> 00:15:16.790
Gibbons loved to play
tennis, and he and Del Rio

00:15:16.790 --> 00:15:19.250
hosted parties like this
one almost every weekend

00:15:19.250 --> 00:15:19.860
at their home.

00:15:27.050 --> 00:15:29.930
Their house was in Santa Monica,
and in fact it's still there.

00:15:29.930 --> 00:15:34.460
It's considered a Los
Angeles historic landmark.

00:15:34.460 --> 00:15:36.500
As we look at a little
buffet that's spread out

00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:37.208
for the guests...

00:15:41.240 --> 00:15:43.160
The woman in the
braids is Rocky Cooper

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:45.890
who is married to Gary Cooper
and was Cedric Gibbons's niece.

00:15:50.050 --> 00:15:51.910
And I don't know what
King Vidor is trying

00:15:51.910 --> 00:15:54.467
to tell us about his teeth.

00:15:54.467 --> 00:15:56.800
You can see a little bit of
the house in the background.

00:15:56.800 --> 00:16:00.520
The style is Streamline Moderne,
and Gibbons helped design

00:16:00.520 --> 00:16:01.770
the interiors for the house.

00:16:04.970 --> 00:16:07.160
Marlene Dietrich who is
good friends with both Del

00:16:07.160 --> 00:16:07.880
Rio and Gibbons.

00:16:14.280 --> 00:16:17.730
And we get one last look at
our hostess, a screen legend

00:16:17.730 --> 00:16:21.210
both in Hollywood and in
Mexico, Dolores Del Rio.

00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:29.900
And now we're headed
up to the Bay Area

00:16:29.900 --> 00:16:32.510
with cinematographer
James Wong Howe.

00:16:32.510 --> 00:16:35.210
Here he is.

00:16:35.210 --> 00:16:38.390
Howe's traveling with
his partner Sanora Babb

00:16:38.390 --> 00:16:41.570
and several of their friends,
Babb's sister Dorothy,

00:16:41.570 --> 00:16:44.450
the novelist James Hilton
and his wife Alice,

00:16:44.450 --> 00:16:50.560
and Hungarian photographer
and actor Charles Korvin.

00:16:50.560 --> 00:16:51.625
And I love this shot.

00:16:51.625 --> 00:16:53.000
It's very interesting
to see what

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:55.370
a professional cinematographer
will do with a home movie

00:16:55.370 --> 00:16:56.360
camera.

00:16:56.360 --> 00:16:58.670
Look at that, the Golden
Gate Bridge peeking out

00:16:58.670 --> 00:17:01.790
of a cloud or perhaps fog.

00:17:01.790 --> 00:17:03.620
The bridge had just
opened a few months

00:17:03.620 --> 00:17:07.790
before this home movie
was shot in May of 1937.

00:17:07.790 --> 00:17:10.069
Here come our
traveling companions.

00:17:10.069 --> 00:17:12.560
This is Sanora Babb
in the fur collar.

00:17:12.560 --> 00:17:16.460
James Hilton is the tall
man in the long coat,

00:17:16.460 --> 00:17:18.470
and that's Alice
in the red scarf

00:17:18.470 --> 00:17:24.130
and Dorothy Babb in the
longer scarf on the far left.

00:17:24.130 --> 00:17:26.630
And I love this, because here's
James Wong Howe getting out

00:17:26.630 --> 00:17:29.470
his light meter to
set up a home movie.

00:17:29.470 --> 00:17:30.720
You don't see that very often.

00:17:39.780 --> 00:17:42.900
Some beautiful shots
of San Francisco

00:17:42.900 --> 00:17:44.557
back in the early days.

00:17:44.557 --> 00:17:46.140
The skyline looks
quite different now.

00:17:53.610 --> 00:17:56.250
And now we're heading out over
the Oakland Bay Bridge which

00:17:56.250 --> 00:18:00.330
had just opened a year before
in 1936 and was at this time

00:18:00.330 --> 00:18:03.460
the longest bridge in the world.

00:18:03.460 --> 00:18:05.370
We're on the top level
which was for cars.

00:18:05.370 --> 00:18:07.740
There was also at that time
a bottom level for trains,

00:18:07.740 --> 00:18:08.740
which was later removed.

00:18:14.470 --> 00:18:16.765
And you can see the angle
where Howe is looking down

00:18:16.765 --> 00:18:18.390
at the people in the
front seat, so you

00:18:18.390 --> 00:18:20.520
can tell he's standing
up in the back seat

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:21.960
to shoot this home movie.

00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:23.520
That's not very common.

00:18:23.520 --> 00:18:26.130
That's another thing that,
as a cinematographer,

00:18:26.130 --> 00:18:27.120
he thought of that.

00:18:27.120 --> 00:18:30.920
But who else had really
come up with that idea?

00:18:30.920 --> 00:18:33.450
He was an extraordinarily
successful cinematographer

00:18:33.450 --> 00:18:35.617
and he's considered really
one of the most important

00:18:35.617 --> 00:18:37.560
cinematographers in
Hollywood history.

00:18:37.560 --> 00:18:40.110
As we look out at
Coit Tower there.

00:18:40.110 --> 00:18:43.440
He started in silent films
and he worked all the way up

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:45.390
into the mid '70s.

00:18:45.390 --> 00:18:47.940
He was nominated for
10 Academy Awards

00:18:47.940 --> 00:18:50.460
and he won twice for The
Rose Tattoo and for Hud.

00:19:07.830 --> 00:19:12.030
He and Sanora Babb were a
couple from the '30s onward,

00:19:12.030 --> 00:19:14.597
but they weren't allowed
to get married at this time

00:19:14.597 --> 00:19:16.430
because there were laws
in the United States

00:19:16.430 --> 00:19:18.110
against interracial marriage.

00:19:18.110 --> 00:19:21.530
They were finally able to marry
in 1948 when California changed

00:19:21.530 --> 00:19:28.990
that law, and they were together
until Howe passed away in 1976.

00:19:28.990 --> 00:19:31.570
That's a good shot of Charles
Korvin in the sunglasses

00:19:31.570 --> 00:19:33.220
and this great old gas station.

00:19:36.410 --> 00:19:38.470
And James Hilton, now
he was a novelist.

00:19:38.470 --> 00:19:42.940
He wrote Lost Horizon, Random
Harvest, Goodbye, Mr. Chips,

00:19:42.940 --> 00:19:45.445
all made into successful
and popular films.

00:19:48.630 --> 00:19:53.510
And this is a typical home movie
gag, oops, we forgot somebody.

00:19:53.510 --> 00:19:57.180
Sanora Babb waves Howe
back into the car.

00:19:57.180 --> 00:19:58.680
They seem to really
love each other.

00:20:03.300 --> 00:20:06.150
Babb had an interesting
life in her own right.

00:20:06.150 --> 00:20:07.400
She was a writer.

00:20:07.400 --> 00:20:08.900
She wrote a really
good novel called

00:20:08.900 --> 00:20:11.670
Whose Names Are Unknown about
her experiences in the Dust

00:20:11.670 --> 00:20:15.160
Bowl and working for the
Farm Security Administration,

00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:18.140
and I really recommend it.

00:20:18.140 --> 00:20:20.630
Now we're in San
Francisco's Chinatown,

00:20:20.630 --> 00:20:23.890
and some beautiful
shots of the buildings.

00:20:23.890 --> 00:20:27.050
Howe actually was born in China
and he came with his family

00:20:27.050 --> 00:20:29.180
to the United States when
he was just a little boy.

00:20:35.750 --> 00:20:36.530
Some cute kids.

00:20:44.720 --> 00:20:47.120
And now we're back on the
road with a paper map.

00:20:47.120 --> 00:20:49.130
Remember those, Randy?

00:20:49.130 --> 00:20:49.940
I still have some.

00:20:51.320 --> 00:20:56.410
So do I. And a beautiful
shot of Sanora Babb.

00:21:03.340 --> 00:21:05.690
And there's Howe in
the side view mirror.

00:21:05.690 --> 00:21:07.100
Let's hope he's not driving.

00:21:12.340 --> 00:21:13.990
Well, that was really
terrific, Lynne,

00:21:13.990 --> 00:21:16.900
and now it's time for
yet another segment.

00:21:16.900 --> 00:21:18.760
This time we've
got a special guest

00:21:18.760 --> 00:21:22.660
to join us, the son of
a legendary director,

00:21:22.660 --> 00:21:25.280
who worked as a behind
the scenes person

00:21:25.280 --> 00:21:27.010
in films for many years himself.

00:21:27.010 --> 00:21:29.050
He was the son of
Henry Koster who

00:21:29.050 --> 00:21:32.500
directed really great classics
like The Bishop's Wife

00:21:32.500 --> 00:21:36.040
and Harvey, and Deanna
Durbin musicals and lots

00:21:36.040 --> 00:21:37.390
of other great films.

00:21:37.390 --> 00:21:41.010
So please welcome Bob Koster.

00:21:41.010 --> 00:21:42.350
Bob, thanks for joining us.

00:21:42.350 --> 00:21:43.770
Oh, it's my pleasure.

00:21:43.770 --> 00:21:45.610
Thank you for having me.

00:21:45.610 --> 00:21:46.580
Hi, Bob.

00:21:46.580 --> 00:21:47.540
Hi, Lynne.

00:21:47.540 --> 00:21:51.230
Bob, your dad was kind of an
interesting guy in that he

00:21:51.230 --> 00:21:54.050
was born in Berlin and ended
up making films in Berlin,

00:21:54.050 --> 00:21:57.320
but then came over to the
United States in the 1930s.

00:21:57.320 --> 00:22:02.720
But he also was a guy who was
not only a consummate filmmaker

00:22:02.720 --> 00:22:04.560
but a consummate entertainer.

00:22:04.560 --> 00:22:09.710
His films had a very humanist,
light, multi-layered touch.

00:22:09.710 --> 00:22:13.860
So let's take a journey with you
and your dad and his home movie

00:22:13.860 --> 00:22:14.360
camera.

00:22:14.360 --> 00:22:16.730
I would love it if Bob
would tell a little story

00:22:16.730 --> 00:22:19.680
about his father leaving
Europe before we roll the film,

00:22:19.680 --> 00:22:21.680
because it's a quite
interesting story about how

00:22:21.680 --> 00:22:24.380
your father came to leave
Europe as the Nazis were coming

00:22:24.380 --> 00:22:25.190
to power.

00:22:25.190 --> 00:22:29.060
Well, Dad knew he would
have to leave Europe.

00:22:29.060 --> 00:22:31.070
He would have to leave
Germany at any rate,

00:22:31.070 --> 00:22:33.620
because Hitler had
just come to power,

00:22:33.620 --> 00:22:38.360
and there were laws against
Jewish people earning a living,

00:22:38.360 --> 00:22:38.960
actually.

00:22:38.960 --> 00:22:42.890
And so he could no
longer direct in Berlin.

00:22:42.890 --> 00:22:45.350
He was just
finishing up a movie.

00:22:45.350 --> 00:22:48.620
He went to the bank to get
his money out so that he could

00:22:48.620 --> 00:22:51.860
pay for the railroad
ticket out of Germany,

00:22:51.860 --> 00:22:55.100
and there was a bank teller
there who the day before

00:22:55.100 --> 00:22:56.510
had been a friend of his.

00:22:56.510 --> 00:22:59.360
Now the fellow was
wearing a Nazi uniform.

00:22:59.360 --> 00:23:02.060
He was an SA officer.

00:23:02.060 --> 00:23:05.810
And Dad said, I'd like
to take my money out.

00:23:05.810 --> 00:23:08.030
He said, what's your name?

00:23:08.030 --> 00:23:12.280
It was the guy who had greeted
him as a friend the day before,

00:23:12.280 --> 00:23:14.510
and Dad said, well,
it's Henry Koster.

00:23:14.510 --> 00:23:16.820
And he said, you don't
have any money here,

00:23:16.820 --> 00:23:18.200
and Dad said, yes, I do.

00:23:18.200 --> 00:23:21.350
And he took his passbook
out of his pocket

00:23:21.350 --> 00:23:28.040
and handed it to the man, and
the man without looking at it

00:23:28.040 --> 00:23:30.440
tore it up and threw
it on the floor.

00:23:30.440 --> 00:23:34.250
Dad was not happy about
that, and Dad said,

00:23:34.250 --> 00:23:36.560
I would like to see the manager.

00:23:36.560 --> 00:23:42.470
And this guard said,
Mr. Schmidt is not here.

00:23:42.470 --> 00:23:44.270
And Dad said, well,
then I'll wait for him,

00:23:44.270 --> 00:23:47.420
and Dad sat down
at the man's desk.

00:23:47.420 --> 00:23:49.610
And they waited a few minutes.

00:23:49.610 --> 00:23:52.820
And the guard realized
Dad wasn't going to leave,

00:23:52.820 --> 00:23:57.320
and he said, hold on a second,
and he picked up the phone.

00:23:57.320 --> 00:23:59.990
He dialed a number.

00:23:59.990 --> 00:24:01.880
He said, hello, Mr Schmidt.

00:24:01.880 --> 00:24:03.890
He said, I have a Jew
here named Koster who

00:24:03.890 --> 00:24:05.960
wants to take his money out.

00:24:05.960 --> 00:24:07.550
OK, I'll tell him.

00:24:07.550 --> 00:24:10.340
And he hung up and he
turned to Dad and he said,

00:24:10.340 --> 00:24:12.980
Mr Schmidt is not here.

00:24:12.980 --> 00:24:15.230
And Dad said, well,
wait a minute,

00:24:15.230 --> 00:24:17.750
you were just talking
to him on the phone.

00:24:17.750 --> 00:24:20.840
And the man made a really
bad mistake at that point.

00:24:20.840 --> 00:24:25.910
He said, you Jew bastard,
you're calling me a liar.

00:24:25.910 --> 00:24:29.120
And Dad lost his
temper and picked up

00:24:29.120 --> 00:24:33.040
the telephone in his hand and
knocked the man out with it.

00:24:33.040 --> 00:24:38.120
And Mr. Schmidt came and
pulled Dad off of the teller

00:24:38.120 --> 00:24:40.880
and hustled him
out the front door

00:24:40.880 --> 00:24:44.120
and took some money
out of his own pocket

00:24:44.120 --> 00:24:46.610
and handed it to
my father and said,

00:24:46.610 --> 00:24:50.690
look, you take this money, and
you go to the railroad station

00:24:50.690 --> 00:24:53.020
and leave the country right now.

00:24:53.020 --> 00:24:56.030
And Dad said, but
I'm on my lunch hour.

00:24:56.030 --> 00:24:56.810
Doesn't matter.

00:24:56.810 --> 00:24:59.330
If you don't do that,
you'll be killed.

00:24:59.330 --> 00:25:02.330
Dad left Germany
on his lunch hour

00:25:02.330 --> 00:25:05.150
with the clothes on his
back, with whatever money

00:25:05.150 --> 00:25:06.993
this man had given him.

00:25:06.993 --> 00:25:07.910
And he went to France.

00:25:07.910 --> 00:25:10.340
He happened to have his
passport in his pocket.

00:25:10.340 --> 00:25:11.720
That's how he left Germany.

00:25:11.720 --> 00:25:13.840
That was 1933.

00:25:13.840 --> 00:25:17.000
He wandered around Europe,
stayed in Paris a little while,

00:25:17.000 --> 00:25:19.160
went to Budapest.

00:25:19.160 --> 00:25:22.340
Re-met, since he had
known him before,

00:25:22.340 --> 00:25:27.950
Joe Pasternak who was working
in Europe for Carl Laemmle,

00:25:27.950 --> 00:25:29.990
the owner of Universal.

00:25:29.990 --> 00:25:34.400
And Pasternak, who knew my
dad, hired him as a director.

00:25:34.400 --> 00:25:36.140
And they did four
movies together.

00:25:36.140 --> 00:25:38.390
After he had made
these movies, Laemmle

00:25:38.390 --> 00:25:42.020
hired him to come to
Hollywood in 1936.

00:25:42.020 --> 00:25:44.810
And the movie that
Dad made, which

00:25:44.810 --> 00:25:48.500
was called Three Smart
Girls with Deanna Durbin,

00:25:48.500 --> 00:25:52.670
and the movie following it
which was 100 Men and A Girl,

00:25:52.670 --> 00:25:56.090
brought Universal
out of bankruptcy.

00:25:56.090 --> 00:25:56.810
It's amazing.

00:25:56.810 --> 00:25:59.810
And that was his first Hollywood
film - Three Smart Girls.

00:25:59.810 --> 00:26:01.130
Three Smart Girls, yeah.

00:26:01.130 --> 00:26:03.380
Well, maybe we should have
a look at these home movies

00:26:03.380 --> 00:26:05.480
when we can see him
actually coming over

00:26:05.480 --> 00:26:07.340
to the United States.

00:26:07.340 --> 00:26:09.064
And we'll go from there.

00:26:09.064 --> 00:26:09.992
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:26:09.992 --> 00:26:13.240
Oh, here we are.

00:26:13.240 --> 00:26:17.170
Those are people waving
him goodbye in Austria

00:26:17.170 --> 00:26:22.930
when he got on the train to
go from Vienna around Germany

00:26:22.930 --> 00:26:25.510
because they couldn't
go through Germany.

00:26:25.510 --> 00:26:28.240
Here, they --
Pasternak and my dad --

00:26:28.240 --> 00:26:31.390
my dad is on the right,
Pasternak on the left --

00:26:31.390 --> 00:26:34.703
on the deck of the ship,
discussing what movies

00:26:34.703 --> 00:26:35.620
they're going to make.

00:26:35.620 --> 00:26:37.630
They were on the Ile De France.

00:26:37.630 --> 00:26:42.040
In those days, one of the
really luxurious cruising ships.

00:26:47.496 --> 00:26:50.000
Of course, they didn't
have cruises in those days.

00:26:50.000 --> 00:26:51.770
They just crossed the ocean.

00:26:51.770 --> 00:26:52.270
Oh, look.

00:26:52.270 --> 00:26:54.760
There's my mother.

00:26:54.760 --> 00:26:57.250
Kitty, Kitty Koster.

00:26:57.250 --> 00:26:59.920
I have no idea
who this lady was.

00:26:59.920 --> 00:27:03.250
But I certainly wish I did.

00:27:03.250 --> 00:27:05.468
She -- she had
the moves, anyway.

00:27:05.468 --> 00:27:06.010
Look at that.

00:27:09.520 --> 00:27:15.490
That's New York when they
arrived on February 21, 1936.

00:27:15.490 --> 00:27:16.450
That's Pasternak.

00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:21.890
That's New York.

00:27:21.890 --> 00:27:26.050
Those are the docks, those
were people waiting for people

00:27:26.050 --> 00:27:27.400
coming on the Ile De France.

00:27:30.100 --> 00:27:32.860
And that's my
father's first house

00:27:32.860 --> 00:27:35.800
on Bronson Avenue in Hollywood.

00:27:35.800 --> 00:27:36.830
Wow, so he made --

00:27:36.830 --> 00:27:39.130
That's my father's
first wife, actually.

00:27:39.130 --> 00:27:40.030
My mother.

00:27:40.030 --> 00:27:43.420
And that gentleman was Mr. Rubin
who was teaching Dad English.

00:27:43.420 --> 00:27:44.650
He didn't speak English.

00:27:44.650 --> 00:27:47.060
So now, we're jumping
ahead to 1949,

00:27:47.060 --> 00:27:49.180
behind the scenes of
Come to the Stable.

00:27:49.180 --> 00:27:53.230
This is Elsa Lanchester
who was the wife

00:27:53.230 --> 00:27:56.320
of Charles Laughton who was
one of Dad's best friends.

00:27:56.320 --> 00:27:59.790
There is Celeste Holm.

00:27:59.790 --> 00:28:02.500
And there is Joe
LaShelle, the cameraman.

00:28:02.500 --> 00:28:07.700
And he did several
pictures with Dad.

00:28:07.700 --> 00:28:09.670
You ever see a nun
smoking before?

00:28:09.670 --> 00:28:10.666
I haven't.

00:28:16.650 --> 00:28:20.365
Celeste Holm, Loretta Young
were the two major nuns

00:28:20.365 --> 00:28:21.715
in the picture.

00:28:21.715 --> 00:28:27.710
And there were a number of
others as you could see.

00:28:27.710 --> 00:28:29.960
So this is behind the scenes.

00:28:29.960 --> 00:28:34.190
This fellow is making snow
because, believe it or not,

00:28:34.190 --> 00:28:38.000
in Beverly Hills, California,
there's no snow in the winter.

00:28:53.670 --> 00:28:54.720
That was Hugh Marlowe.

00:28:54.720 --> 00:28:56.670
This is Thomas Gomez.

00:28:56.670 --> 00:28:58.010
And the lady's name --

00:28:58.010 --> 00:28:59.020
Marion Martin.

00:28:59.020 --> 00:29:00.210
Marion Martin.

00:29:00.210 --> 00:29:02.400
She plays the manicurist.

00:29:02.400 --> 00:29:08.650
And here is obviously Loretta
Young who needs a manicure.

00:29:08.650 --> 00:29:09.930
Oh, I guess not.

00:29:09.930 --> 00:29:10.890
She's doing all right.

00:29:14.820 --> 00:29:15.410
Oh, look.

00:29:15.410 --> 00:29:19.310
There's my stepmother, Peggy
Moran, with Celeste Holm.

00:29:19.310 --> 00:29:21.970
And Celeste is bouncing
a tennis racket.

00:29:21.970 --> 00:29:24.170
And there is Loretta
Young with her daughter.

00:29:24.170 --> 00:29:25.440
What was her name again?

00:29:25.440 --> 00:29:25.940
Judy.

00:29:25.940 --> 00:29:26.550
Judy.

00:29:26.550 --> 00:29:27.050
Yeah.

00:29:27.050 --> 00:29:30.520
And that is the daughter of
Clark Gable with Loretta Young.

00:29:30.520 --> 00:29:33.670
Although, you're not
supposed to know that.

00:29:33.670 --> 00:29:36.160
And there's Hugh
Marlowe with his wife.

00:29:36.160 --> 00:29:37.810
Yeah, K.T. Stevens.

00:29:37.810 --> 00:29:39.270
K.T. Stevens, of course.

00:29:43.140 --> 00:29:45.580
Joe LaShelle, trying
to sneak off the set.

00:29:45.580 --> 00:29:46.330
He failed.

00:29:49.760 --> 00:29:54.330
And his assistant, this
gentleman, was Ray Wise Mala.

00:29:54.330 --> 00:29:56.430
He was born in Alaska.

00:29:56.430 --> 00:29:58.020
And he was the
first Alaska native

00:29:58.020 --> 00:30:01.980
to work as a cameraman
in Hollywood.

00:30:01.980 --> 00:30:03.450
He also did quite
a bit of acting

00:30:03.450 --> 00:30:06.540
in the 1930s and early 1940s.

00:30:06.540 --> 00:30:09.150
And then he decided he was
better off as a cameraman.

00:30:09.150 --> 00:30:11.280
So he became a cameraman.

00:30:11.280 --> 00:30:13.265
And he was very good at it.

00:30:23.460 --> 00:30:26.560
And there is Celeste Holm
bouncing up and down.

00:30:30.490 --> 00:30:33.040
And there's Ray, Ray Mala.

00:30:33.040 --> 00:30:35.420
He worked with LaShelle
on the film Laura.

00:30:35.420 --> 00:30:38.900
And also, he worked on Shadow
of a Doubt, the Hitchcock film.

00:30:38.900 --> 00:30:44.150
Well, LaShelle was kind of his
mentor in doing camerawork.

00:30:44.150 --> 00:30:45.290
A very nice man.

00:30:45.290 --> 00:30:46.400
I remember him.

00:30:46.400 --> 00:30:51.290
And his grandson, Ted Mala
is still a very good friend.

00:30:51.290 --> 00:30:52.850
He lives in Anchorage.

00:30:52.850 --> 00:30:54.290
There is Loretta Young again.

00:30:57.420 --> 00:31:01.590
There is my father with
his scarf and dark glasses.

00:31:01.590 --> 00:31:03.250
There's some lights
in the background

00:31:03.250 --> 00:31:05.770
that you never see those
kind of lights anymore.

00:31:05.770 --> 00:31:08.620
Those are arcs.

00:31:08.620 --> 00:31:12.170
They use them to fill in.

00:31:12.170 --> 00:31:14.190
There's Dad being
proud of himself,

00:31:14.190 --> 00:31:18.330
and rightly so, I might say.

00:31:18.330 --> 00:31:21.390
And there's Celeste.

00:31:21.390 --> 00:31:25.330
How many times have you
seen nuns playing tennis?

00:31:25.330 --> 00:31:26.560
And she was good at it, too.

00:31:30.997 --> 00:31:34.230
And in a moment you
will see Louis Jean --

00:31:34.230 --> 00:31:36.570
Heydt or Heydt?

00:31:36.570 --> 00:31:39.600
I think it's Heydt,
but I'm not positive.

00:31:39.600 --> 00:31:41.490
Heydt is better than Heydt, OK.

00:31:41.490 --> 00:31:41.990
[CHUCKLES]

00:31:41.990 --> 00:31:43.310
Anyway--

00:31:43.310 --> 00:31:44.790
And that's Dorothy Patrick.

00:31:44.790 --> 00:31:45.900
Oh, Dorothy Patrick.

00:31:45.900 --> 00:31:47.970
That's right.

00:31:47.970 --> 00:31:49.910
Everybody's doing their
nails at this picture.

00:31:52.580 --> 00:31:53.660
Look at those cameras.

00:31:53.660 --> 00:31:55.700
Now, everybody
carries phones, huh.

00:31:58.270 --> 00:32:00.530
Yeah, that was a good picture.

00:32:00.530 --> 00:32:01.780
And it was in black and white.

00:32:01.780 --> 00:32:06.480
So it's great to see this color
footage behind the scenes.

00:32:06.480 --> 00:32:08.100
That was really fantastic.

00:32:08.100 --> 00:32:10.170
Thanks so much,
Bob, for joining us.

00:32:10.170 --> 00:32:14.970
And thanks for depositing these
films with the Academy Film

00:32:14.970 --> 00:32:17.400
Archive and allowing us to
share them with the world.

00:32:17.400 --> 00:32:20.820
It's really terrific to have
the films of a director who

00:32:20.820 --> 00:32:23.070
not only enjoyed
doing home movies,

00:32:23.070 --> 00:32:27.190
but behind the scenes films as
well where we can see that he

00:32:27.190 --> 00:32:30.300
had his different family and
a separate family on the set

00:32:30.300 --> 00:32:31.430
when he made his films.

00:32:31.430 --> 00:32:33.520
That familial feeling
really comes across.

00:32:33.520 --> 00:32:35.100
So thanks again.

00:32:35.100 --> 00:32:38.480
Well, the Academy, of course,
is where the films belong.

00:32:38.480 --> 00:32:39.300
Absolutely.

00:32:39.300 --> 00:32:43.390
So that anybody who's
interested can go and see them,

00:32:43.390 --> 00:32:45.660
and they become part
of your history.

00:32:45.660 --> 00:32:47.280
Thanks so much, Bob.

00:32:47.280 --> 00:32:49.010
My pleasure.

00:32:49.010 --> 00:32:51.020
So now we're moving on
to our next home movie.

00:32:51.020 --> 00:32:52.160
And this one's very short.

00:32:52.160 --> 00:32:54.140
So I wanted to tell you
a little bit about it

00:32:54.140 --> 00:32:55.370
before we watch it.

00:32:55.370 --> 00:32:58.220
This footage was shot by
writer-director Richard Brooks

00:32:58.220 --> 00:33:02.660
in November of 1948 at an
exhibition baseball game.

00:33:02.660 --> 00:33:05.390
Brooks has captured one
of the all-time baseball

00:33:05.390 --> 00:33:08.390
greats, the legendary
Satchel Paige

00:33:08.390 --> 00:33:10.430
pitching for a team of
barnstorming Negro League

00:33:10.430 --> 00:33:11.470
players.

00:33:11.470 --> 00:33:14.810
And several of those players
are members of the famed Kansas

00:33:14.810 --> 00:33:16.130
City Monarchs.

00:33:16.130 --> 00:33:18.710
Paige was only the
seventh Negro Leaguer

00:33:18.710 --> 00:33:20.930
to join a Major League team.

00:33:20.930 --> 00:33:24.530
He signed with the Cleveland
Indians in the summer of 1948.

00:33:24.530 --> 00:33:27.050
And he helped Cleveland to
win the pennant and the World

00:33:27.050 --> 00:33:28.440
Series that year.

00:33:28.440 --> 00:33:31.280
Now the Negro Leagues were
formed because the Major

00:33:31.280 --> 00:33:33.320
Leagues were segregated.

00:33:33.320 --> 00:33:35.000
Negro Leagues were
very important

00:33:35.000 --> 00:33:37.220
in the African-American
community.

00:33:37.220 --> 00:33:40.310
But there's very little motion
picture footage of them.

00:33:40.310 --> 00:33:42.500
The white newsreels
didn't cover them

00:33:42.500 --> 00:33:45.260
and Hollywood didn't
make films about them.

00:33:45.260 --> 00:33:48.080
So these home movie
images are very rare.

00:33:48.080 --> 00:33:50.270
All the more so because
they're in color.

00:33:50.270 --> 00:33:52.466
So let's have a look.

00:33:52.466 --> 00:33:54.660
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:33:54.660 --> 00:33:56.880
Here we go to Wrigley
Field, Los Angeles,

00:33:56.880 --> 00:34:00.240
the minor league ballpark
in LA at that time, home

00:34:00.240 --> 00:34:03.520
of the Pacific Coast League.

00:34:03.520 --> 00:34:05.470
And here comes Satchel
Paige to the mound.

00:34:08.210 --> 00:34:10.810
He's wearing his
Cleveland uniform

00:34:10.810 --> 00:34:13.570
while his teammates are
wearing their Royals uniforms.

00:34:13.570 --> 00:34:16.030
That was the name of
their barnstorming team.

00:34:16.030 --> 00:34:17.830
That's Louis B.
Mayer on the left,

00:34:17.830 --> 00:34:21.449
and MGM musical producer
Arthur Freed on the right.

00:34:27.003 --> 00:34:28.420
Number 30 walking
out to the mound

00:34:28.420 --> 00:34:32.889
is Gene Bearden who was a rookie
phenom for Cleveland in 1948.

00:34:32.889 --> 00:34:35.820
And his team is called the Bob
Lemon Gene Bearden All Stars.

00:34:35.820 --> 00:34:38.320
They're a team of Major Leaguers
playing against the Royals.

00:34:42.900 --> 00:34:45.390
So, beautiful shot of
the ballpark by Brooks.

00:34:53.110 --> 00:34:58.073
And there's Paige
pitching the ball.

00:34:58.073 --> 00:34:59.740
And that might not
seem like a big deal,

00:34:59.740 --> 00:35:01.870
but I have never seen
any other footage

00:35:01.870 --> 00:35:04.150
of Paige pitching in a game.

00:35:04.150 --> 00:35:06.938
I hope there's some out there,
and I hope some day we find it.

00:35:06.938 --> 00:35:08.980
But in the meantime, it
goes to show how valuable

00:35:08.980 --> 00:35:10.450
a home movie like this is.

00:35:13.510 --> 00:35:15.550
I love these women,
very dressed up.

00:35:15.550 --> 00:35:17.675
I don't usually dress up
quite so much myself when

00:35:17.675 --> 00:35:18.550
I go to the ballpark.

00:35:23.640 --> 00:35:25.960
So Paige is pitching the
first game of a double header,

00:35:25.960 --> 00:35:28.512
and he wins his game
against Gene Bearden.

00:35:28.512 --> 00:35:30.220
And then they move on
to the second game,

00:35:30.220 --> 00:35:31.607
and the Royals lose that one.

00:35:31.607 --> 00:35:32.440
So they even it out.

00:35:36.070 --> 00:35:38.530
There's producer Bill Goetz
with some other spectators

00:35:38.530 --> 00:35:39.370
in the background.

00:35:43.840 --> 00:35:45.535
The MGM folk may be
here because they

00:35:45.535 --> 00:35:47.702
were working on a film right
now called The Stratton

00:35:47.702 --> 00:35:50.110
Story with James Stewart,
which is a baseball themed

00:35:50.110 --> 00:35:52.120
film about Monty Stratton.

00:35:52.120 --> 00:35:54.760
And Gene Bearden was
actually in that film.

00:35:54.760 --> 00:36:00.202
He has a part as one of the
teammates of Jimmy Stewart's.

00:36:00.202 --> 00:36:01.910
Mayer seems to be
quite enjoying himself.

00:36:06.480 --> 00:36:12.380
There's Gene Bearden in the
gray sitting with the MGM folks

00:36:12.380 --> 00:36:14.895
and signing an autograph.

00:36:14.895 --> 00:36:16.270
And now we get
the Goodyear Blimp

00:36:16.270 --> 00:36:17.980
trailing an advertising banner.

00:36:17.980 --> 00:36:18.860
And that's the end.

00:36:23.360 --> 00:36:25.200
And now, we're heading
up to San Francisco

00:36:25.200 --> 00:36:27.420
for the premiere of China Gate.

00:36:27.420 --> 00:36:31.370
This is the Fox Theater
on Market Street.

00:36:31.370 --> 00:36:34.250
And the film was
directed by Sam Fuller.

00:36:34.250 --> 00:36:37.610
It was also written and
produced by Sam Fuller.

00:36:37.610 --> 00:36:41.890
And he got 20th Century
Fox to distribute it.

00:36:41.890 --> 00:36:43.840
Given that it was not a
big studio production,

00:36:43.840 --> 00:36:45.610
the premiere is a
little more kind

00:36:45.610 --> 00:36:48.820
of home grown than your
typical Hollywood premiere.

00:36:48.820 --> 00:36:50.950
So we have some local
talent to entertain

00:36:50.950 --> 00:36:53.590
the premiere attendees here.

00:36:56.370 --> 00:36:59.580
Sam Fuller often made his films
outside the typical studio

00:36:59.580 --> 00:37:00.720
system.

00:37:00.720 --> 00:37:02.370
And that way, he got
to make the films

00:37:02.370 --> 00:37:07.030
he wanted to make as opposed to
what he would have been given.

00:37:07.030 --> 00:37:09.180
So sadly, Angie Dickinson
wasn't able to make it

00:37:09.180 --> 00:37:09.880
to the premiere.

00:37:09.880 --> 00:37:12.030
But both Gene Barry
and Nat King Cole

00:37:12.030 --> 00:37:15.090
show up in a couple of minutes.

00:37:15.090 --> 00:37:17.730
Nat King Cole had made a
number of films prior to this.

00:37:17.730 --> 00:37:21.270
But he always played the part
of a singer or played himself.

00:37:21.270 --> 00:37:22.890
And in this film,
he actually got

00:37:22.890 --> 00:37:24.780
to play an actual acting role.

00:37:24.780 --> 00:37:26.400
He played a soldier.

00:37:26.400 --> 00:37:28.350
And he was probably
drawn to the part

00:37:28.350 --> 00:37:30.630
because the film had
an anti-racist theme.

00:37:34.650 --> 00:37:37.170
Cole, of course, was already
a hugely popular singer

00:37:37.170 --> 00:37:39.840
at this time.

00:37:39.840 --> 00:37:42.780
In the course of his career,
he had 100 different songs

00:37:42.780 --> 00:37:45.900
or more really that
were on the pop charts.

00:37:45.900 --> 00:37:48.270
He also, at this time, was
making a television series

00:37:48.270 --> 00:37:49.999
called The Nat King Cole Show.

00:37:54.310 --> 00:37:55.930
So here come our
celebrity guests.

00:37:55.930 --> 00:37:58.300
That's Sam Fuller in
front of the limo.

00:37:58.300 --> 00:38:01.200
That's Maria Cole who just
got out with a white wrap.

00:38:01.200 --> 00:38:03.790
Gene Barry getting out
now with his wife, Betty.

00:38:03.790 --> 00:38:04.960
And here's Nat King Cole...

00:38:07.590 --> 00:38:09.015
Posing for some publicity shots.

00:38:11.980 --> 00:38:13.750
Maria Cole was also a singer.

00:38:13.750 --> 00:38:17.190
And of course, her
daughter with Nat King Cole

00:38:17.190 --> 00:38:20.940
is Natalie Cole, another
very famous singer.

00:38:20.940 --> 00:38:23.170
Gene Barry may be
best known for playing

00:38:23.170 --> 00:38:26.740
Bat Masterson in that TV
series and had many other film

00:38:26.740 --> 00:38:28.960
and television credits.

00:38:28.960 --> 00:38:30.708
He's also in the Fuller
film, Forty Guns.

00:38:30.708 --> 00:38:32.500
And some of the other
actors from that film

00:38:32.500 --> 00:38:34.570
come to the premiere as
well to support Fuller.

00:38:38.360 --> 00:38:42.550
So Cole goes over to talk
to some of the little girls.

00:38:42.550 --> 00:38:44.910
And I think he's just
very sweet about this.

00:38:44.910 --> 00:38:46.380
You can tell he's
asking her name,

00:38:46.380 --> 00:38:48.547
and he doesn't quite hear
it and he asked her again.

00:38:54.912 --> 00:38:56.802
And Gene Barry lifts
one of them up.

00:38:56.802 --> 00:38:59.260
I hope she's enjoying that as
much as it looks like she is.

00:39:07.030 --> 00:39:10.030
Nat King Cole takes a
turn with the baton.

00:39:10.030 --> 00:39:10.930
He's very talented.

00:39:10.930 --> 00:39:11.470
Why not?

00:39:18.120 --> 00:39:21.570
And now we've added George
Givot on the far left.

00:39:21.570 --> 00:39:22.800
He's also in this film.

00:39:22.800 --> 00:39:26.520
And he started out in the early
days in the Ziegfeld Follies.

00:39:26.520 --> 00:39:28.530
Probably best known for
being the voice of Luigi

00:39:28.530 --> 00:39:30.690
in The Lady and the Tramp.

00:39:30.690 --> 00:39:34.080
That's Barry Sullivan
who's in Forty Guns,

00:39:34.080 --> 00:39:37.440
and also Eve Brent in
the stoll, and Robert Dix

00:39:37.440 --> 00:39:39.690
next to her, both in
Forty Guns as well.

00:39:44.660 --> 00:39:46.177
And we don't know
who this lady is,

00:39:46.177 --> 00:39:48.260
but she sure looks like
she belongs at a Hollywood

00:39:48.260 --> 00:39:51.270
premiere, doesn't she?

00:39:51.270 --> 00:39:56.670
So in they go to see the
premiere of China Gate.

00:39:56.670 --> 00:39:59.640
It's so good to see
this footage of Nat King

00:39:59.640 --> 00:40:01.860
Cole, wonderful talent.

00:40:01.860 --> 00:40:05.760
And I'm equally excited for
our next home movie clip

00:40:05.760 --> 00:40:10.620
because it features one of
my absolute favorite stars.

00:40:10.620 --> 00:40:13.890
It's footage from the set of
The Trouble With Angels shot

00:40:13.890 --> 00:40:16.620
by Gypsy Rose Lee and --

00:40:16.620 --> 00:40:18.420
who is also in the film.

00:40:18.420 --> 00:40:24.210
But we're going to be joined
today by our star of the film.

00:40:24.210 --> 00:40:27.630
Please welcome one of my
very favorite actresses

00:40:27.630 --> 00:40:30.900
for my whole life, Hayley Mills.

00:40:30.900 --> 00:40:31.670
Thank you, Randy.

00:40:31.670 --> 00:40:33.950
It's lovely to
join you for this.

00:40:33.950 --> 00:40:35.560
I'm looking forward to it.

00:40:35.560 --> 00:40:36.060
Me, too.

00:40:36.060 --> 00:40:39.960
And I realize this
was a long time ago.

00:40:39.960 --> 00:40:41.440
You were just a kid.

00:40:41.440 --> 00:40:43.590
And at the same
time, hopefully, this

00:40:43.590 --> 00:40:45.090
will bring back some memories.

00:40:45.090 --> 00:40:48.240
Yes, I hope -- well,
it is a long time ago.

00:40:48.240 --> 00:40:49.860
I was 19.

00:40:49.860 --> 00:40:53.190
But to have this
footage jogs the memory.

00:40:53.190 --> 00:40:56.160
I just wanted to ask you a
few questions about Trouble

00:40:56.160 --> 00:40:58.320
With Angels and
the film before we

00:40:58.320 --> 00:40:59.660
start looking at this footage.

00:40:59.660 --> 00:41:03.470
So you grew up in a family where
this was the family business.

00:41:03.470 --> 00:41:05.040
You were in acting.

00:41:05.040 --> 00:41:07.350
You had your father who
was a well-known actor,

00:41:07.350 --> 00:41:09.840
your mother was an
actor but also a writer.

00:41:09.840 --> 00:41:12.420
And of course, your
sister, as well.

00:41:12.420 --> 00:41:14.820
How did you guys
juggle your schedules?

00:41:14.820 --> 00:41:19.080
Well, it was quite a headache
for my mother because hitherto,

00:41:19.080 --> 00:41:22.020
she had always gone
wherever my dad was working.

00:41:22.020 --> 00:41:24.630
And then I started working.

00:41:24.630 --> 00:41:27.420
So then she was split
between being with him

00:41:27.420 --> 00:41:29.010
and being with me.

00:41:29.010 --> 00:41:33.060
But then Disney arranged
for me to have a guardian.

00:41:33.060 --> 00:41:35.490
So that released my mother.

00:41:35.490 --> 00:41:37.240
She didn't have
to sit on the set.

00:41:37.240 --> 00:41:41.010
She wasn't really cut out
to be a stage mom at all.

00:41:41.010 --> 00:41:42.780
So that was much better.

00:41:42.780 --> 00:41:44.720
I was 19.

00:41:44.720 --> 00:41:48.210
And I really didn't need
to have my mom anymore.

00:41:48.210 --> 00:41:52.170
So I -- it was the first
time that I went to Hollywood

00:41:52.170 --> 00:41:53.090
on my own.

00:41:53.090 --> 00:41:56.400
The production gave me the
most incredible trailer.

00:41:56.400 --> 00:42:00.940
It was literally the
length of a city block.

00:42:00.940 --> 00:42:04.410
It was huge and empty.

00:42:04.410 --> 00:42:06.210
It had an enormous
kitchen and everything.

00:42:06.210 --> 00:42:11.340
I remember I used to buy those
ready-made packets of dough

00:42:11.340 --> 00:42:14.460
which you chopped up into little
bits and put them in the oven

00:42:14.460 --> 00:42:17.100
and they turned into biscuits.

00:42:17.100 --> 00:42:18.660
And I would take
them on to the set.

00:42:18.660 --> 00:42:20.800
That's about the only
cooking I ever did.

00:42:20.800 --> 00:42:23.020
So I was there on
my own by that time,

00:42:23.020 --> 00:42:26.760
which was quite an experience.

00:42:26.760 --> 00:42:30.300
That's really terrific to think
of you passing out biscuits.

00:42:30.300 --> 00:42:32.550
Your sister was
working on Broadway

00:42:32.550 --> 00:42:35.130
when you were starting
out and received a Tony

00:42:35.130 --> 00:42:37.260
nomination for her performance.

00:42:37.260 --> 00:42:40.530
Shortly after that time, you
received a Juvenile Oscar

00:42:40.530 --> 00:42:42.900
for your performance
in Pollyanna.

00:42:42.900 --> 00:42:45.900
So your parents suddenly
had these two award winning

00:42:45.900 --> 00:42:48.060
actresses in their family.

00:42:48.060 --> 00:42:49.310
What was that like?

00:42:49.310 --> 00:42:50.730
Well, it was very exciting.

00:42:50.730 --> 00:42:54.330
When I got mine, I was 14.

00:42:54.330 --> 00:42:58.740
I think it took a few years
for it to really sink in.

00:42:58.740 --> 00:43:03.660
I don't think I really fully
appreciated the relevance

00:43:03.660 --> 00:43:05.430
until I was older.

00:43:05.430 --> 00:43:08.010
And of course, I didn't
go to the ceremony.

00:43:08.010 --> 00:43:10.420
I think if I had
gone to the ceremony,

00:43:10.420 --> 00:43:13.410
it would have had
more of an impact.

00:43:13.410 --> 00:43:16.970
When I was at school,
in my boarding school,

00:43:16.970 --> 00:43:21.870
I think my parents decided
that it would probably

00:43:21.870 --> 00:43:25.530
have gone straight to my head
and turned me into a brat.

00:43:25.530 --> 00:43:26.640
So --

00:43:26.640 --> 00:43:28.260
Let's watch this footage.

00:43:28.260 --> 00:43:30.190
And you can tell us some
of your experiences.

00:43:30.190 --> 00:43:31.607
And I'll have a
few more questions

00:43:31.607 --> 00:43:34.340
that we can talk a
little bit after.

00:43:34.340 --> 00:43:35.280
Yes.

00:43:35.280 --> 00:43:37.710
This is footage from the
Gypsy Rose Lee Collection

00:43:37.710 --> 00:43:40.950
featuring you, Hayley, Rosalind
Russell, and director Ida

00:43:40.950 --> 00:43:42.300
Lupino.

00:43:42.300 --> 00:43:43.696
Smile quietly.

00:43:43.696 --> 00:43:44.570
[CHUCKLES]

00:43:44.570 --> 00:43:47.280
So Mother Superior
was the working title.

00:43:47.280 --> 00:43:50.070
And here we go with
actress Rosalind Russell.

00:43:50.070 --> 00:43:52.610
Roz -- she was great.

00:43:52.610 --> 00:43:54.990
She was a very
impressive person.

00:43:54.990 --> 00:43:59.250
Beautiful and impressive.

00:43:59.250 --> 00:44:01.530
And Binnie Barnes, yeah.

00:44:01.530 --> 00:44:02.180
Marvelous.

00:44:08.770 --> 00:44:11.365
There's Ida Lupino
directing away.

00:44:11.365 --> 00:44:13.305
Oh.

00:44:13.305 --> 00:44:18.690
Ida -- Yes.

00:44:18.690 --> 00:44:21.660
Probably very good
I didn't decide

00:44:21.660 --> 00:44:24.020
to become a percussionist.

00:44:24.020 --> 00:44:26.190
I don't think I had a
great talent for it.

00:44:31.430 --> 00:44:33.190
There's your co-star,
June Harding.

00:44:38.782 --> 00:44:39.720
[CHUCKLES]

00:44:39.720 --> 00:44:41.320
June -- she was so good.

00:44:41.320 --> 00:44:42.365
Such a sweet girl.

00:44:45.260 --> 00:44:48.000
This is at the Greystone
Mansion in Los Angeles.

00:44:48.000 --> 00:44:49.350
Oh, gosh.

00:44:49.350 --> 00:44:50.070
Yes.

00:44:50.070 --> 00:44:51.760
All that fake snow.

00:44:51.760 --> 00:44:54.060
If you sneezed, it went
straight up your nose.

00:44:54.060 --> 00:44:54.810
[GASPS]

00:44:54.810 --> 00:44:56.890
Oh, and there's Mary Wickes.

00:44:56.890 --> 00:44:58.140
She's so wonderful.

00:44:58.140 --> 00:44:58.740
I loved her.

00:45:12.030 --> 00:45:15.370
The Mother Superior
having a crafty cigarette.

00:45:19.010 --> 00:45:22.132
I love June's bouffant
underneath that hoodie.

00:45:22.132 --> 00:45:22.632
Yes.

00:45:22.632 --> 00:45:24.576
[CHUCKLES]

00:45:33.810 --> 00:45:37.520
They had to keep putting this
white powder onto our backsides

00:45:37.520 --> 00:45:39.755
because we're supposed
to have fallen down.

00:45:39.755 --> 00:45:41.130
But of course,
the more you walk,

00:45:41.130 --> 00:45:44.490
the more the powder fell off
which was quite funny, really,

00:45:44.490 --> 00:45:47.940
particularly since it was
probably about 90 degrees heat.

00:45:47.940 --> 00:45:51.540
And the whole crew were
sweltering and wearing

00:45:51.540 --> 00:45:53.530
Hawaiian shirts.

00:45:53.530 --> 00:45:57.390
And we're having fake
snow put all over us.

00:45:57.390 --> 00:45:59.000
That's show business, I guess.

00:46:24.590 --> 00:46:26.420
Oh, here we are.

00:46:26.420 --> 00:46:28.651
Yes, Gypsy Rose Lee.

00:46:35.390 --> 00:46:38.630
I was thrilled to
meet her, actually.

00:46:38.630 --> 00:46:40.330
It was extraordinary.

00:46:40.330 --> 00:46:44.080
I never thought I would
meet Gypsy because I'd

00:46:44.080 --> 00:46:45.670
seen her film by this time.

00:46:48.946 --> 00:46:53.830
And here she was, standing
in front of me in the flesh.

00:46:53.830 --> 00:46:57.770
She was glamorous, and very
sweet, and funny, and very,

00:46:57.770 --> 00:46:59.890
very down to earth.

00:46:59.890 --> 00:47:02.840
I think that was part
of her great appeal

00:47:02.840 --> 00:47:06.930
and why she was
so very successful

00:47:06.930 --> 00:47:11.670
as a striptease artist is that
she seemed to sort of keep it

00:47:11.670 --> 00:47:13.450
at a distance.

00:47:13.450 --> 00:47:18.120
But I mean, she never
looked down on her audience.

00:47:18.120 --> 00:47:21.460
But she didn't seem to take
it 100% seriously either.

00:47:21.460 --> 00:47:23.600
She always had a sense
of humor about it.

00:47:27.940 --> 00:47:30.500
The footage here where you're
dancing with the scarves

00:47:30.500 --> 00:47:32.170
is not in the film.

00:47:32.170 --> 00:47:36.810
So this is some outtakes
we're able to see here.

00:47:36.810 --> 00:47:39.650
Because what I really
wanted Gypsy to do

00:47:39.650 --> 00:47:42.605
was teach me her
burlesque routine.

00:47:46.700 --> 00:47:47.970
Yeah, you had two --

00:47:47.970 --> 00:47:51.840
well, three really remarkable
women on this film with

00:47:51.840 --> 00:47:55.200
Rosalind Russell,
Gypsy Rose Lee --

00:47:55.200 --> 00:47:58.390
who had achieved so
much in her life.

00:47:58.390 --> 00:48:00.210
She was a very
successful businesswoman.

00:48:00.210 --> 00:48:01.950
She was a very good writer.

00:48:01.950 --> 00:48:04.530
And Ida Lupino.

00:48:04.530 --> 00:48:11.190
I think she was one of the only,
if not the only, female film

00:48:11.190 --> 00:48:14.880
director at that time in
Hollywood, which is really

00:48:14.880 --> 00:48:19.820
saying something in an industry
largely dominated by men.

00:48:26.440 --> 00:48:28.960
And Ida seems to be
directing everybody

00:48:28.960 --> 00:48:31.780
here very specifically on
what she wants people to do.

00:48:31.780 --> 00:48:36.220
She was very decisive, very
sure, very absolutely clear

00:48:36.220 --> 00:48:37.450
about what she wanted.

00:48:37.450 --> 00:48:39.760
There was no sort
of faffing around.

00:48:59.560 --> 00:49:02.580
Ida -- she always
looked wonderful.

00:49:02.580 --> 00:49:05.430
Bill Frye, our
producer, suggested

00:49:05.430 --> 00:49:10.818
that she wore only black
and white on the set.

00:49:10.818 --> 00:49:11.860
Sometimes, she wore hats.

00:49:19.420 --> 00:49:21.718
Ah, I wish I knew what she said.

00:49:21.718 --> 00:49:23.010
Obviously made everybody laugh.

00:49:25.550 --> 00:49:26.060
Oh, yes.

00:49:26.060 --> 00:49:30.360
And Ida used to insist that
we all call her Mother.

00:49:30.360 --> 00:49:33.680
And on the back of her chair
was written Mother Of Us All.

00:49:37.110 --> 00:49:42.270
Yes, three remarkable
women, strong women,

00:49:42.270 --> 00:49:43.650
clever, talented women.

00:49:48.130 --> 00:49:51.470
And here comes another mother.

00:49:51.470 --> 00:49:54.950
And of course, everybody
in their nun's habit

00:49:54.950 --> 00:49:56.650
just suits everybody.

00:49:56.650 --> 00:50:00.200
And it focused you on
the essential things

00:50:00.200 --> 00:50:03.940
about a person, their face,
their eyes, their smile.

00:50:03.940 --> 00:50:05.870
There's something
wonderful about that.

00:50:10.010 --> 00:50:11.840
June's birthday.

00:50:11.840 --> 00:50:13.180
I think that candle was --

00:50:13.180 --> 00:50:16.772
they probably stole
that off the altar.

00:50:16.772 --> 00:50:17.980
I don't know how old she was.

00:50:17.980 --> 00:50:19.250
I think she was --

00:50:19.250 --> 00:50:20.760
she was certainly older than me.

00:50:20.760 --> 00:50:21.340
Yeah.

00:50:21.340 --> 00:50:25.510
I think she was about 28, about
almost 10 years older than you.

00:50:25.510 --> 00:50:27.380
She didn't seem like it.

00:50:27.380 --> 00:50:29.000
She had a youthful energy.

00:50:29.000 --> 00:50:30.454
Yes, she did.

00:50:50.150 --> 00:50:53.570
I wish I could lip read and
know what we were talking about.

00:50:56.534 --> 00:50:58.020
[LAUGHS]

00:50:58.020 --> 00:50:58.740
She was great.

00:50:58.740 --> 00:51:01.320
She had such gaiety.

00:51:01.320 --> 00:51:03.170
Gypsy's holding a
small microphone there.

00:51:03.170 --> 00:51:05.240
She'd bring along her
small portable tape

00:51:05.240 --> 00:51:07.850
recorder and microphone
because not only would she

00:51:07.850 --> 00:51:11.240
shoot home movies, she
would record sound.

00:51:11.240 --> 00:51:13.220
She'd take that wild
sound and add it

00:51:13.220 --> 00:51:16.022
to the home movies later
along with little interviews.

00:51:16.022 --> 00:51:18.230
And sometimes, she'd edit
them together and show them

00:51:18.230 --> 00:51:24.376
on her very popular television
show, The Gypsy Rose Lee Show

00:51:24.376 --> 00:51:25.760
Oh.

00:51:25.760 --> 00:51:28.635
A bit challenging
after all that cake.

00:51:37.352 --> 00:51:39.336
[CHUCKLES]

00:51:41.840 --> 00:51:45.930
Oh, it's lovely to see this.

00:51:45.930 --> 00:51:46.430
Lovely.

00:51:48.990 --> 00:51:51.280
That was really fun to hear
you talk about it, too.

00:51:51.280 --> 00:51:55.140
And especially about the
strong women on the set.

00:51:55.140 --> 00:51:58.530
Could you talk a little bit
about Ida Lupino's directing

00:51:58.530 --> 00:51:59.370
style?

00:51:59.370 --> 00:52:02.790
Having been an actress,
she let everybody

00:52:02.790 --> 00:52:04.740
do what they wanted to do.

00:52:04.740 --> 00:52:08.970
She saw what they had
brought to the set

00:52:08.970 --> 00:52:11.900
and then make suggestions.

00:52:11.900 --> 00:52:14.880
Some film directors,
surprisingly, leave it up

00:52:14.880 --> 00:52:19.260
to the actors and they're
interested more in

00:52:19.260 --> 00:52:21.140
where they're going
to put the camera

00:52:21.140 --> 00:52:25.410
or how they're going to cut the
scene, their overall vision.

00:52:25.410 --> 00:52:28.710
So they leave what the actors
are doing up to the actors.

00:52:28.710 --> 00:52:31.170
And then you've
got directors who

00:52:31.170 --> 00:52:34.280
give so much attention
to actors and leave

00:52:34.280 --> 00:52:39.240
all of the technical things
to their cameraman and editor.

00:52:39.240 --> 00:52:41.970
And Ida had a very
good idea of both.

00:52:41.970 --> 00:52:47.190
She certainly made me feel
confident, and I trusted her.

00:52:47.190 --> 00:52:50.340
It seemed odd to have
a woman as a director

00:52:50.340 --> 00:52:54.570
because in my experience,
it had always been a man.

00:52:54.570 --> 00:52:56.350
Now, suddenly, it's a woman.

00:52:56.350 --> 00:52:58.170
And it was a little odd.

00:52:58.170 --> 00:52:59.920
And then I began to enjoy it.

00:52:59.920 --> 00:53:01.500
I really appreciated it.

00:53:01.500 --> 00:53:02.970
I really liked her.

00:53:02.970 --> 00:53:07.140
And that sort of changed
my perspective, I suppose,

00:53:07.140 --> 00:53:11.790
about what was the woman's
role, what was the man's role.

00:53:11.790 --> 00:53:15.280
And then here is this very
successful woman director

00:53:15.280 --> 00:53:19.860
who didn't have to
deny her femininity.

00:53:19.860 --> 00:53:23.040
She didn't try to be manly.

00:53:23.040 --> 00:53:26.550
She wasn't -- she didn't
wear suits and march about,

00:53:26.550 --> 00:53:28.540
which sounds like a
silly thing to say,

00:53:28.540 --> 00:53:30.600
but she was just herself.

00:53:30.600 --> 00:53:33.270
And I think that's
why looking back,

00:53:33.270 --> 00:53:36.020
it's rather sweet and
rather clever of her

00:53:36.020 --> 00:53:38.910
to say that everybody
had to call her Mother.

00:53:38.910 --> 00:53:41.670
Everybody else except
the cast on the set

00:53:41.670 --> 00:53:45.880
were men, except the makeup
and the hair and the wardrobe

00:53:45.880 --> 00:53:46.750
department.

00:53:46.750 --> 00:53:49.330
So if you're calling
the director Mother,

00:53:49.330 --> 00:53:51.772
you're going to be a
little bit more respectful.

00:53:51.772 --> 00:53:53.070
[CHUCKLES]

00:53:53.070 --> 00:53:54.510
They've all got mothers.

00:53:54.510 --> 00:53:56.220
But I think it was quite clever.

00:53:56.220 --> 00:53:57.600
I'm Mother of you all.

00:53:57.600 --> 00:53:59.340
You know, I'm not
challenging you,

00:53:59.340 --> 00:54:01.140
I'm not competing with you.

00:54:01.140 --> 00:54:03.750
So one of the interesting
things about Ida Lupino

00:54:03.750 --> 00:54:08.610
is when she was an
actress in the early '30s,

00:54:08.610 --> 00:54:11.610
she already had a career
going in Hollywood.

00:54:11.610 --> 00:54:15.660
But she had contracted polio,
and her contract with Paramount

00:54:15.660 --> 00:54:18.300
was dropped because
of her illness.

00:54:18.300 --> 00:54:20.400
During that time when
she thought maybe

00:54:20.400 --> 00:54:22.770
her acting career
might be over, she

00:54:22.770 --> 00:54:26.130
realized that while
intellectually she was fine,

00:54:26.130 --> 00:54:27.780
she could still
write, it was then

00:54:27.780 --> 00:54:30.060
that she began thinking
about directing.

00:54:30.060 --> 00:54:35.130
Really, that kind of difficulty
made her re-evaluate and then

00:54:35.130 --> 00:54:39.330
ended up creating the
pathway for the only director

00:54:39.330 --> 00:54:43.340
in the Directors Guild who
was a woman in the '50s was

00:54:43.340 --> 00:54:44.190
Ida Lupino.

00:54:44.190 --> 00:54:45.900
I think it's really
terrific that there

00:54:45.900 --> 00:54:49.860
is an even more poignant part
to the story above and beyond

00:54:49.860 --> 00:54:51.750
fighting for a position.

00:54:51.750 --> 00:54:53.020
That's very interesting.

00:54:53.020 --> 00:54:53.970
I didn't know that.

00:54:53.970 --> 00:54:56.640
And of course, it
was at a time when

00:54:56.640 --> 00:55:01.210
women were beginning to really
challenge the status quo.

00:55:01.210 --> 00:55:05.850
So she was definitely one of the
first, as was Gypsy Rose Lee.

00:55:05.850 --> 00:55:08.700
I think one of the interesting
things about this film

00:55:08.700 --> 00:55:11.850
is that it was so
female-centric in a time

00:55:11.850 --> 00:55:13.770
when that wasn't
really very common.

00:55:13.770 --> 00:55:16.200
And yet at the same
time, there were examples

00:55:16.200 --> 00:55:18.210
and there were people
pushing the boundaries.

00:55:18.210 --> 00:55:18.710
So --

00:55:18.710 --> 00:55:19.470
Yeah.

00:55:19.470 --> 00:55:24.640
My big disappointment
with the film --

00:55:24.640 --> 00:55:26.800
when I first read the
script, I remember

00:55:26.800 --> 00:55:30.460
being absolutely thrilled
that I was finally going

00:55:30.460 --> 00:55:33.130
to get into a nun's habit.

00:55:33.130 --> 00:55:35.110
Because like so many --

00:55:35.110 --> 00:55:39.330
I'm sure like so
many girls, there's

00:55:39.330 --> 00:55:44.770
something terribly romantic
about being a bride of Christ

00:55:44.770 --> 00:55:49.140
and a life of contemplation
and public service,

00:55:49.140 --> 00:55:54.170
and apart from anything, wearing
all those wonderful things.

00:55:54.170 --> 00:55:58.110
And I did think about becoming
a nun, actually, for a while.

00:55:58.110 --> 00:56:01.960
Then when we got to
the film, and they

00:56:01.960 --> 00:56:04.300
decided they wouldn't
go to the point

00:56:04.300 --> 00:56:07.030
where she actually
dons the habit,

00:56:07.030 --> 00:56:08.950
I was terribly disappointed.

00:56:08.950 --> 00:56:10.440
But anyway, there we are.

00:56:10.440 --> 00:56:12.221
You can't have everything.

00:56:12.221 --> 00:56:14.930
[CHUCKLING]

00:56:14.930 --> 00:56:16.630
And you're writing
your memoir, I hear.

00:56:16.630 --> 00:56:18.272
So that's really fantastic.

00:56:18.272 --> 00:56:19.230
I look forward to that.

00:56:19.230 --> 00:56:20.300
Yes, I am.

00:56:20.300 --> 00:56:20.800
I am.

00:56:20.800 --> 00:56:23.730
So you've stirred up
the old phagocytes,

00:56:23.730 --> 00:56:27.070
so thank you very much.

00:56:27.070 --> 00:56:30.090
A pleasure.

00:56:30.090 --> 00:56:32.910
For our last segment, we've
got a very special treat.

00:56:32.910 --> 00:56:35.130
One of the other things
that the Academy has

00:56:35.130 --> 00:56:36.540
is an Oral History Program.

00:56:36.540 --> 00:56:38.760
We've been shooting
interviews with a lot

00:56:38.760 --> 00:56:41.670
of people from all various
branches of the Academy.

00:56:41.670 --> 00:56:44.880
It's really fantastic to
have their memories recorded.

00:56:44.880 --> 00:56:49.560
We realized that we had a
very special oral history, one

00:56:49.560 --> 00:56:54.150
with Gregory Nava where Greg
not only talked about growing up

00:56:54.150 --> 00:56:58.650
in San Diego, but also narrated
some of the home movies

00:56:58.650 --> 00:57:02.530
and watched them with our
moderator at that time.

00:57:02.530 --> 00:57:07.320
So we're going to show these
with the very special sound

00:57:07.320 --> 00:57:10.440
of a projector in the
background, which I

00:57:10.440 --> 00:57:12.390
think is very nostalgic for me.

00:57:12.390 --> 00:57:14.430
If you have a home
movie projector,

00:57:14.430 --> 00:57:16.440
you'll probably recognize
what's going on.

00:57:16.440 --> 00:57:20.160
And at the same time, you'll
hear his live reaction

00:57:20.160 --> 00:57:24.014
to these films and
the people in them.

00:57:24.014 --> 00:57:26.798
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:57:29.120 --> 00:57:32.330
When I grew up, my
parents were always

00:57:32.330 --> 00:57:34.670
taking home movies, 8
millimeter home movies.

00:57:34.670 --> 00:57:37.880
My whole life is on 8
millimeter home movies.

00:57:37.880 --> 00:57:40.267
So let's take a look at
some of these images,

00:57:40.267 --> 00:57:42.350
and some of the people who
we'll be talking about.

00:57:42.350 --> 00:57:44.060
This is me.

00:57:44.060 --> 00:57:46.070
I think it's my third birthday.

00:57:46.070 --> 00:57:48.100
Happy Birthday, Greg.

00:57:48.100 --> 00:57:49.750
And that's my older
brother, John.

00:57:49.750 --> 00:57:53.960
And he, of course, has become
a very, very famous painter.

00:57:53.960 --> 00:57:56.630
He did all of the tapestries
on the inside of the cathedral

00:57:56.630 --> 00:57:58.700
in downtown Los Angeles.

00:57:58.700 --> 00:58:02.930
And there I am on the
right, the younger brother.

00:58:02.930 --> 00:58:04.760
And that's our little
house in San Diego.

00:58:04.760 --> 00:58:06.140
We were very poor.

00:58:06.140 --> 00:58:08.930
And we lived in a
little clapboard house

00:58:08.930 --> 00:58:10.683
behind another house, right?

00:58:10.683 --> 00:58:11.610
[CHUCKLES]

00:58:11.610 --> 00:58:13.640
There's my mom.

00:58:13.640 --> 00:58:15.530
We didn't have a lot of
money, but she always

00:58:15.530 --> 00:58:17.822
made sure that we were always
clean, very well dressed.

00:58:17.822 --> 00:58:19.250
She was always
very well dressed.

00:58:19.250 --> 00:58:22.940
There, you have a really
nice look at our little house

00:58:22.940 --> 00:58:24.950
that I was raised in as a child.

00:58:27.560 --> 00:58:29.270
And there's my
grandmother, Sebastiana.

00:58:29.270 --> 00:58:32.210
She was Basque
from the Pyrenees.

00:58:32.210 --> 00:58:35.510
And we were very, very close --

00:58:35.510 --> 00:58:37.627
the family, all of us were.

00:58:37.627 --> 00:58:38.960
And she was a great, great lady.

00:58:38.960 --> 00:58:41.150
And there's my
grandfather in the middle.

00:58:41.150 --> 00:58:45.380
Aita, we called him, which
means father in Basque.

00:58:45.380 --> 00:58:49.070
And you see the man
in the Hawaiian shirt

00:58:49.070 --> 00:58:50.030
is named Paul Nelson.

00:58:50.030 --> 00:58:52.250
That's my father
with Paul Nelson.

00:58:52.250 --> 00:58:54.290
And they were buddies
in World War II.

00:58:54.290 --> 00:58:56.898
There's so many Latinos
fought in the war,

00:58:56.898 --> 00:58:58.190
but their story was never told.

00:58:58.190 --> 00:59:01.490
The first time a Latino
GI story was ever told

00:59:01.490 --> 00:59:03.088
was on my TV show
American Family,

00:59:03.088 --> 00:59:04.380
and that's the reason I did it.

00:59:04.380 --> 00:59:08.600
And this is my brother and I.
And we're going down to Mexico

00:59:08.600 --> 00:59:12.020
to my grandfather's ranch.

00:59:12.020 --> 00:59:14.690
That's my uncle
John with my dad.

00:59:14.690 --> 00:59:18.140
And this is my uncle Martin
who is the great villain

00:59:18.140 --> 00:59:20.960
of the family, who was
characterized in the film

00:59:20.960 --> 00:59:24.140
A Time of Destiny
played by William Hurt.

00:59:24.140 --> 00:59:26.630
And there we are going
down to the ranch

00:59:26.630 --> 00:59:32.150
with the beautiful matilija
poppies that would grow wild.

00:59:32.150 --> 00:59:34.610
This is us at the
Descanso Ranch having

00:59:34.610 --> 00:59:37.070
a barbecue with my family.

00:59:37.070 --> 00:59:39.530
You see my cousin Pammy
there with that red hair.

00:59:39.530 --> 00:59:40.970
That's very Basque.

00:59:40.970 --> 00:59:43.220
And Melissa Leo, that's
one of the reasons

00:59:43.220 --> 00:59:46.850
I picked her for the role of
Josie in A Time of Destiny

00:59:46.850 --> 00:59:50.870
because she had that brilliant
strawberry blonde hair.

00:59:50.870 --> 00:59:53.600
That's my Aunt Jessie
on the right there.

00:59:53.600 --> 00:59:57.020
And there's always a
character in all of my films

00:59:57.020 --> 00:59:58.820
that's based on my Aunt Jessie.

00:59:58.820 --> 01:00:00.200
That's Toyah.

01:00:00.200 --> 01:00:01.640
She was married
to my uncle John.

01:00:01.640 --> 01:00:03.740
She's indigena, from Colima.

01:00:03.740 --> 01:00:08.240
And you see all this mixture of
peoples that were at the ranch.

01:00:08.240 --> 01:00:11.210
And I based a lot of
characters on Toyah.

01:00:11.210 --> 01:00:14.860
She was a very strong
woman, very resilient.

01:00:14.860 --> 01:00:17.220
If you asked my
father what he was,

01:00:17.220 --> 01:00:19.220
what was his most important
thing, he would say,

01:00:19.220 --> 01:00:20.900
I'm a father.

01:00:20.900 --> 01:00:27.190
He was raised without a
father, because in the late

01:00:27.190 --> 01:00:30.882
'20s and early '30s, there was
this tremendous thing that is

01:00:30.882 --> 01:00:33.340
very little dealt with -- and
I deal with it in the film Mi

01:00:33.340 --> 01:00:36.005
Familia, and this is why I dealt
with it in the film Mi Familia

01:00:36.005 --> 01:00:38.860
-- where they had
the deportations,

01:00:38.860 --> 01:00:43.800
where they blamed the Depression
on Mexican people who were

01:00:43.800 --> 01:00:47.400
taking away jobs
from real Americans.

01:00:47.400 --> 01:00:50.130
My Mexican grandfather
was an American citizen.

01:00:50.130 --> 01:00:52.830
They took his citizenship
away, his papers at the border,

01:00:52.830 --> 01:00:54.555
and he was sent back to Mexico.

01:00:54.555 --> 01:00:56.430
He disappeared.

01:00:56.430 --> 01:00:58.770
And my father was
raised without a father.

01:00:58.770 --> 01:01:02.100
And so that, to him, he want --
that was more important to him

01:01:02.100 --> 01:01:05.200
that he wanted to be a
really wonderful father.

01:01:05.200 --> 01:01:07.860
And that's another
reason why, in my films,

01:01:07.860 --> 01:01:11.350
there's very positive
father figures.

01:01:11.350 --> 01:01:12.600
There's my dad.

01:01:12.600 --> 01:01:13.170
Look at him.

01:01:13.170 --> 01:01:15.025
Such a handsome
beautiful man, huh?

01:01:17.640 --> 01:01:19.120
Look at them.

01:01:19.120 --> 01:01:21.660
There's my grandmother.

01:01:21.660 --> 01:01:25.060
She never liked to
have her picture taken.

01:01:25.060 --> 01:01:27.210
And there's my Aunt
Lucy, and she's pregnant.

01:01:27.210 --> 01:01:29.730
You see how my Aita's
playing the saxophone,

01:01:29.730 --> 01:01:30.660
being a real clown.

01:01:30.660 --> 01:01:32.850
And there's my Uncle
Vale from Tijuana.

01:01:32.850 --> 01:01:34.080
And he's playing the trumpet.

01:01:34.080 --> 01:01:36.510
And he owned the car
dealership in Tijuana.

01:01:36.510 --> 01:01:38.310
He was very wealthy.

01:01:38.310 --> 01:01:42.480
And my cousins Valito and Josie.

01:01:42.480 --> 01:01:45.720
And my Aunt Lucy, and she was
so beautiful, my Aunt Lucy.

01:01:45.720 --> 01:01:48.990
She reminded me of
like Evita because she

01:01:48.990 --> 01:01:51.870
would come to our house
in furs and in diamonds.

01:01:51.870 --> 01:01:54.630
In our family, the Mexican
family was the wealthy family,

01:01:54.630 --> 01:01:56.190
and we were poor.

01:01:56.190 --> 01:01:58.620
You can see that these --

01:01:58.620 --> 01:02:02.580
the women of my family are very
powerful and very assertive,

01:02:02.580 --> 01:02:03.900
aren't they?

01:02:03.900 --> 01:02:06.210
Incredible.

01:02:06.210 --> 01:02:08.080
And it's so sad.

01:02:08.080 --> 01:02:11.085
One of the great
tragedies of my family.

01:02:11.085 --> 01:02:12.210
You see my Aunt Lucy there.

01:02:12.210 --> 01:02:13.470
She's pregnant.

01:02:13.470 --> 01:02:18.030
She died two weeks after
this movie was taken.

01:02:18.030 --> 01:02:21.030
And looking at these movies,
when I saw that film that you

01:02:21.030 --> 01:02:23.820
just saw, and I
saw my Aunt Lucy --

01:02:23.820 --> 01:02:26.250
and I hadn't seen her and
thought of her for so many

01:02:26.250 --> 01:02:27.070
years --

01:02:27.070 --> 01:02:29.790
all of these things
came back to me.

01:02:29.790 --> 01:02:35.340
And I realized how much the
things that happened to me with

01:02:35.340 --> 01:02:39.420
my childhood, the contrast
between these worlds of Mexico

01:02:39.420 --> 01:02:44.490
and the United States, the
personal tragedies around

01:02:44.490 --> 01:02:49.650
people like my Aunt Lucy,
like my grandfather,

01:02:49.650 --> 01:02:51.480
the war experiences
of my father,

01:02:51.480 --> 01:02:55.920
my uncle Martin who was such
a bad guy, all these faces,

01:02:55.920 --> 01:02:56.730
and the names --

01:02:56.730 --> 01:03:00.030
Irene, Tony, they're all
the names of my aunts

01:03:00.030 --> 01:03:01.170
and my cousins --

01:03:01.170 --> 01:03:04.860
and I saw all these faces
looking at me from this movie.

01:03:04.860 --> 01:03:10.140
And I was seeing all the
characters of my films.

01:03:17.540 --> 01:03:19.360
Wow, that is just so wonderful.

01:03:19.360 --> 01:03:22.300
Such wonderful stories
and wonderful home movies.

01:03:22.300 --> 01:03:23.800
Thank you, Gregory Nava.

01:03:23.800 --> 01:03:27.040
To think that little boy that
we see with his birthday cake

01:03:27.040 --> 01:03:30.670
at the beginning growing up
to be the director of El Norte

01:03:30.670 --> 01:03:34.990
and Selena and Mi Familia
is really terrific.

01:03:34.990 --> 01:03:37.910
So, really happy we have that.

01:03:37.910 --> 01:03:41.620
So we promised you
not only to show

01:03:41.620 --> 01:03:44.410
you some really rare and
unusual Hollywood Home Movies,

01:03:44.410 --> 01:03:47.260
but we also promised
to tell you about how

01:03:47.260 --> 01:03:51.730
to save and preserve and
share your own home movies.

01:03:51.730 --> 01:03:54.400
So here is one of
the Academy Film

01:03:54.400 --> 01:03:57.040
Archive's archivists,
Sean Savage,

01:03:57.040 --> 01:04:01.090
to tell you a little bit about
how to deal with your home

01:04:01.090 --> 01:04:02.440
movie collection.

01:04:05.660 --> 01:04:06.680
I'm Sean.

01:04:06.680 --> 01:04:08.750
I'm an archivist at the
Academy Film Archive.

01:04:08.750 --> 01:04:11.810
And I've been working with
home movies at home lately.

01:04:11.810 --> 01:04:13.520
But that's my job.

01:04:13.520 --> 01:04:15.940
You may have home movies
that are your own.

01:04:15.940 --> 01:04:17.690
Of course, if you have
a working projector

01:04:17.690 --> 01:04:19.482
and you want to run
them from time to time,

01:04:19.482 --> 01:04:20.960
that's great since
that's why they

01:04:20.960 --> 01:04:22.940
were made in the first place.

01:04:22.940 --> 01:04:25.490
But more likely, you've probably
encountered family films

01:04:25.490 --> 01:04:27.620
from a previous
generation, and you may not

01:04:27.620 --> 01:04:29.410
know what to do with them.

01:04:29.410 --> 01:04:32.720
They might be in cans or boxes
or could be different gages,

01:04:32.720 --> 01:04:36.020
16 or 8 millimeter, Super 8
-- the main thing is that film

01:04:36.020 --> 01:04:37.230
likes to be cool and dry.

01:04:37.230 --> 01:04:39.522
So if you've got a closet
where the temperature doesn't

01:04:39.522 --> 01:04:41.490
fluctuate much,
that's probably fine.

01:04:41.490 --> 01:04:45.410
But a hot attic or the
garage or a dank basement

01:04:45.410 --> 01:04:46.280
would not be good.

01:04:46.280 --> 01:04:47.990
And film likes to breathe.

01:04:47.990 --> 01:04:50.600
So keep them individually
in containers

01:04:50.600 --> 01:04:53.000
that aren't totally airtight.

01:04:53.000 --> 01:04:55.420
These are fancy archival
cans that we use.

01:04:55.420 --> 01:04:57.720
And these were designed
to circulate air.

01:04:57.720 --> 01:05:01.910
But you know, the original box
or can should be fine as long

01:05:01.910 --> 01:05:04.130
as it's not totally
sealed, which

01:05:04.130 --> 01:05:08.420
means we probably want to
take them out of plastic bags

01:05:08.420 --> 01:05:09.830
if you find them like this.

01:05:09.830 --> 01:05:11.660
And sometimes, you'll
find rubber bands

01:05:11.660 --> 01:05:13.125
wrapped around the film.

01:05:13.125 --> 01:05:14.750
And that's not good
because that's just

01:05:14.750 --> 01:05:17.850
going to dry out and
leave a mess over time.

01:05:17.850 --> 01:05:21.110
So the best thing to do is
find some artist tape and just

01:05:21.110 --> 01:05:23.727
tape the ends down that way.

01:05:23.727 --> 01:05:26.060
A lot of people had their
home movies transferred to VHS

01:05:26.060 --> 01:05:27.320
in the 1980s.

01:05:27.320 --> 01:05:29.240
And if you look at
them now, they probably

01:05:29.240 --> 01:05:30.700
don't look so great.

01:05:30.700 --> 01:05:32.930
The speed could be wrong
or the image might even

01:05:32.930 --> 01:05:34.310
be flipped horizontally.

01:05:34.310 --> 01:05:36.110
You may have had
better luck with DVDs

01:05:36.110 --> 01:05:38.480
in the 1990s or 2000s.

01:05:38.480 --> 01:05:42.150
But in any case, never
throw your home movies away.

01:05:42.150 --> 01:05:44.600
There's always going to
be a better technology

01:05:44.600 --> 01:05:46.520
to get the most
from the film image.

01:05:46.520 --> 01:05:48.130
And your original
reels are probably

01:05:48.130 --> 01:05:50.690
going remain more stable
than any format you

01:05:50.690 --> 01:05:52.070
transfer them to.

01:05:52.070 --> 01:05:54.050
If you have no use for
film reels at the house,

01:05:54.050 --> 01:05:56.120
you can donate
them to an archive.

01:05:56.120 --> 01:05:58.310
This is not a pitch,
but at the Academy,

01:05:58.310 --> 01:06:00.950
we collect all kinds
of amateur films,

01:06:00.950 --> 01:06:03.230
not just those of movie stars.

01:06:03.230 --> 01:06:05.720
We're particularly interested
in things that document life

01:06:05.720 --> 01:06:07.247
in Southern California.

01:06:07.247 --> 01:06:09.080
But wherever you live,
there's a good chance

01:06:09.080 --> 01:06:12.320
that there's a regional archive
or historical society that

01:06:12.320 --> 01:06:14.612
may be interested in them.

01:06:14.612 --> 01:06:16.820
I think the best way to
re-engage with home movies is

01:06:16.820 --> 01:06:17.995
Home Movie Day.

01:06:17.995 --> 01:06:20.120
And these are events that
happen all over the world

01:06:20.120 --> 01:06:22.160
usually in October.

01:06:22.160 --> 01:06:24.950
And they invite you to bring
your reels in and watch them

01:06:24.950 --> 01:06:26.720
with other people
in your community.

01:06:26.720 --> 01:06:28.790
You'll find moving
image archivists on hand

01:06:28.790 --> 01:06:30.500
to help guide you.

01:06:30.500 --> 01:06:33.140
I recommend going to
homemovieday.com to find out

01:06:33.140 --> 01:06:34.700
what's happening near you.

01:06:34.700 --> 01:06:37.100
And this is also the site for
the Center for Home Movies.

01:06:37.100 --> 01:06:39.620
And there, you'll find
resources on everything

01:06:39.620 --> 01:06:42.763
you need to know about
what to do with your reels.

01:06:42.763 --> 01:06:43.680
Well, thank you, Sean.

01:06:43.680 --> 01:06:44.760
That was terrific.

01:06:44.760 --> 01:06:47.160
And I hope everybody out
there with home movies

01:06:47.160 --> 01:06:49.800
got a few good ideas about
how to take care of them,

01:06:49.800 --> 01:06:52.410
and to remember to always
keep your original films.

01:06:52.410 --> 01:06:54.840
Now, we'd like to wrap
things up by thanking

01:06:54.840 --> 01:06:58.020
all of our collaborators
and contributors

01:06:58.020 --> 01:06:59.680
and special guests.

01:06:59.680 --> 01:07:00.180
Randy?

01:07:00.180 --> 01:07:00.840
Yes.

01:07:00.840 --> 01:07:03.030
Well, I also want
to say thank you

01:07:03.030 --> 01:07:06.060
to Sean Savage for bringing the
special guest of the hula dog

01:07:06.060 --> 01:07:08.940
because you can't have
a Hollywood Home Movie

01:07:08.940 --> 01:07:12.270
show without some
trained canines involved

01:07:12.270 --> 01:07:13.320
as far as I'm concerned.

01:07:13.320 --> 01:07:16.810
Anyway, thank you, Lynne,
for curating this whole show.

01:07:16.810 --> 01:07:18.390
I'd like to thank
Michael Mortilla

01:07:18.390 --> 01:07:21.000
for bringing the musical
accompaniment to bring them

01:07:21.000 --> 01:07:22.290
to life.

01:07:22.290 --> 01:07:25.500
I'd like to thank Bob
Koster and Sean Savage

01:07:25.500 --> 01:07:27.960
for presenting special
segments, as well as Gregory

01:07:27.960 --> 01:07:30.930
Nava, the director, showing
his family's movies,

01:07:30.930 --> 01:07:33.690
and of course, our special
guest star, Hayley Mills.

01:07:33.690 --> 01:07:35.760
Thank you very much,
Hayley, for joining us.

01:07:35.760 --> 01:07:37.710
And, Lynne, I hope
you're already

01:07:37.710 --> 01:07:40.110
thinking about what Part
Two is going to be like.

01:07:40.110 --> 01:07:42.030
Thanks so much.

01:07:42.030 --> 01:07:43.020
Thank you.

01:07:43.020 --> 01:07:44.150
Bye, Everybody.

01:07:44.150 --> 01:07:45.350
Bye!

01:07:45.350 --> 01:07:48.400
[MUSIC PLAYING]

